/* THE VERDICT - Return to Courtroom Frame */
/* Approximate page count: 1.5-2 pages */
/* Power arc: EXPOSED -> ACCEPTED */
/* This is the LANDING. Full circle from cold open. */

INT. NAVY JAG COURTROOM - DAY

CLOSE ON: Danny's face.

The same face. The same eyes. But different now. The scratches
have faded to thin white lines. Scars that will never fully
disappear. Marks of something that actually happened.

MATCH CUT FROM: The surrender. Danny on his knees in the
headlights, arms raised, the raccoon somewhere behind him
in the chaos.

TO: Danny standing at attention. Dress whites. Collar hiding
most of the damage. What shows only makes him look more human.

COMMANDER WELLS (O.S.)
Petty Officer Vega.

Danny blinks. Returns from wherever he was.

WIDER: The courtroom. Same fluorescent lights. Same wood
paneling. Same sterile formality we saw in the cold open.

But now we know what led here. Every choice. Every failure.
Every moment of a night that should not have happened.

Commander Wells stands before him. Her expression has shifted
from incredulity to something closer to exhaustion. She has
heard it all now. She still does not quite believe it.

COMMANDER WELLS (CONT'D)
The court has reviewed the evidence.
Your testimony. The... documentation.

She glances at the evidence table. The laminated card with
the paw print. Prosecution's Exhibit Fourteen. It sits there
like an accusation with tiny fingers.

COMMANDER WELLS (CONT'D)
Do you have anything to say before
sentencing?

IN THE GALLERY:

Gerald sits rigid. Sport coat. Wrong tie. His jaw works the
way it does when he is trying not to speak. Trying not to
fix something that cannot be fixed.

Margaret's hand finds his. Squeezes.

Gerald's eyes are wet. He knows what is coming. He has known
since the letter arrived. Since before that, maybe. Since
the first time Danny said "I'm fine" and Gerald heard "I'm
drowning."

BACK TO DANNY:

He stands at attention. The posture of a man trained to
take whatever comes next.

For a moment, his eyes find Gerald's across the room. Father
and son. A lifetime of missed connections. Of love expressed
in casseroles and worry expressed in silence.

Danny's mouth opens.

We expect something. An explanation. A defense. The speech
where the protagonist makes meaning of the chaos.

DANNY
No, ma'am.

Two words. That is all.

But they carry everything: acceptance, accountability, the
end of fighting something that was never meant to be fought.

Commander Wells nods. She expected more. She got what she
needed.

COMMANDER WELLS
Very well.

She turns to the bench. The JUDGE, face unseen, looms in
the periphery of the frame.

COMMANDER WELLS (CONT'D)
(formal, reciting)
On the charges of fraudulent
manipulation of court-mandated
sobriety monitoring equipment,
driving under the influence on
federal property, and conduct
unbecoming...

She continues, but the AUDIO FADES as we--

PUSH IN ON: Gerald's face.

His expression tells us everything. The sentence is severe.
His boy is going away.

A single tear tracks down Gerald's weathered cheek. Margaret's
grip tightens.

BACK TO: Danny.

Standing at attention as the words wash over him. He does
not flinch. He does not break. He simply... accepts.

This is what acceptance looks like. Not defeat. Not
resignation. Clarity.

HARD CUT TO:

INT. NAVY JAG COURTROOM - MOMENTS LATER

The room empties. Shuffling feet. Murmured conversations.

Danny is led toward a side door by TWO SHORE PATROL OFFICERS.

He pauses. Turns back.

Gerald stands alone in the gallery. Margaret has stepped
away, giving them this moment.

Father and son look at each other.

GERALD
(barely audible)
Danny--

DANNY
(cutting him off, gentle)
It's okay, Dad.

Beat.

DANNY (CONT'D)
I'm okay.

This time, when he says it, we believe him.

Danny turns. Walks through the door.

It closes behind him.

HOLD ON: Gerald. Standing alone in the empty courtroom.

Then--

HARD CUT TO:

A NEWSPAPER.

CLOSE ON: The headline, filling the frame.

"NAVY INVESTIGATES 'WILDLIFE OVERRIDE' LOOPHOLE
IN BREATHALYZER SYSTEMS"

Below the headline: a small graphic. A RACCOON PAW PRINT,
stylized, like a logo for the strangest scandal the military
has ever seen.

The byline credits an investigation sparked by "the Vega
incident."

FREEZE FRAME on the newspaper.

TITLE CARD (over the freeze frame):

Following the Vega case, the Navy overhauled
breathalyzer interlock protocols across all
installations.

The "Wildlife Override" loophole was closed.

Danny Vega was dishonorably discharged.
He now works as an addiction counselor
in San Diego.

He has been sober for three years.

The headline holds. A drunk legacy. Policy changed by the
worst decision a man ever made.

FADE TO BLACK.

Silence.

Then--

Faint, almost imperceptible: the CHITTER of a raccoon.

EXT. ALLEY - DUMPSTER - NIGHT

A single sodium vapor light. A dumpster. The same cathedral
glow.

TWO EYES catch the light from the darkness. Reflecting.
Watching.

A pause.

Then the eyes disappear into shadow.

FADE TO BLACK.

/* End scene - approximately 2 pages */
